<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daulat Rao Scindia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daulat Rao Scindia was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Gwalior from 1794 to 1827, successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a> as head of the Scindia family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a>. Defeated by the British at Assaye and Laswari in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>, he ceded territory by the Treaty of Surji-Anjangaon in 1803. He later settled his army camp, the Lashkar, at Gwalior, which became the state's capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daulat-rao-scindia.jpg" alt="Daulat Rao Scindia" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAHARAJA_DAULAT_RAO_SCINDIA_OF_GWALIOR_(R._1794-1827).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Daulat Rao was a grandson of Tukoji Rao Scindia, elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahadji-scindia">Mahadji Scindia</a>, who was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> on 7 January 1761. When Mahadji died without an heir he took the Gwalior throne on 12 February 1794, aged 15; the Satara Chhatrapati and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> installed him on 3 March, and Shah Alam II gave him the titles Naib Vakil-i-Mutlaq and Amir-al-Umara on 10 May. He inherited the modern army Mahadji had built under Benoît de Boigne, and saw himself less as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> than as the chief sovereign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The death of Peshwa Madhavrao II in 1795, the rise of Yashwantrao Holkar and the intrigues of Nana Farnavis let him gain the ascendancy in the confederacy. Under the influence of Sarjerao Ghatge, whose daughter he married in 1798, he seized lands from the Ponwars of Dhar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dewas">Dewas</a>. In July 1801 Yashwantrao extorted a large sum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>; in October Ghatge sacked Indore with every form of atrocity; and in 1802 at Hadapsar, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, Yashwantrao defeated the armies of Scindia and Peshwa Bajirao II, opening the gardi-ka-wakt, a period of unrest in which armies and Pindari bands overran central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>By the Treaty of Bassein of 31 December 1802 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> as the paramount power, and Scindia's evasions brought him into war with the British and defeats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, Assaye, Argaon, Asirgarh and Laswari. He lost his lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, South Haryana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a>, and the fortresses of Gwalior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohad">Gohad</a>, which were restored in 1805 with the Chambal as his northern boundary. He conquered Chanderi in 1811 and signed the Treaty of Gwalior in 1817, promising help against the Pindaris, and in 1818 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> and other lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Daulat Rao, who also bore the title the defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, ruled Gwalior until his death on 21 March 1827. His kingdom kept its independence longer than most Indian states: in 1832 a British parliamentary committee reported that within the Peninsula Sindhia was the only prince who preserved the semblance of independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daulat Rao Sindhia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Daulat Rao Scindia</b> lost the Second Anglo-Maratha War in 1803 and moved his military camp, Lashkar, to Gwalior.</blockquote><a href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Gwalior, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.21,78.18&amp;year=1810" aria-label="Gwalior on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6614 1201 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Daulat Rao Scindia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7064 1471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=26.21,78.18&amp;year=1810">What was here in 1810? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
